    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        Computing Tree Structures in Anonymous Graphs via Mobile <span class="search-hit mathjax">Agents</span>
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Chand%2C+P+K">Prabhat Kumar Chand</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Kumar%2C+M">Manish Kumar</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Molla%2C+A+R">Anisur Rahaman Molla</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="search-hit">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2506.19365v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        &hellip;computing, traditionally studied in the message-passing model, where static nodes communicate via messages. This paper investigates MST and BFS tree construction in an <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span>-based network, where mobile&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2506.19365v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2506.19365v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2506.19365v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        Minimum Spanning Tree (MST) and Breadth-First Search (BFS) tree constructions are classical problems in distributed computing, traditionally studied in the message-passing model, where static nodes communicate via messages. This paper investigates MST and BFS tree construction in an <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span>-based network, where mobile <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> explore a graph and compute. Each node hosts one <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span>, and communication occurs when <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> meet at a node. We consider $n$ <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> initially dispersed (one per node) in an anonymous, arbitrary $n$-node, $m$-edge graph $G$. The goal is to construct the BFS and MST trees from this configuration such that each tree edge is known to at least one of its endpoints, while minimizing time and <span class="search-hit mathjax">memory</span> per <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span>. We work in a synchronous model and assume <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> have no prior knowledge of any graph parameters such as $n$, $m$, $D$, $Δ$ (graph diameter and maximum degree). Prior work solves BFS in $O(DΔ)$ rounds with $O(\log n)$ bits per <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span>, assuming the root is known. We give a deterministic algorithm that constructs the BFS tree in $O(\min(DΔ, m\log n) + n\log n + Δ\log^2 n)$ rounds using $O(\log n)$ bits per <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> without root knowledge. To determine the root, we solve leader election and MST construction. We elect a leader and construct the MST in $O(n\log n + Δ\log^2 n)$ rounds, with $O(\log n)$ bits per <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span>. Prior MST algorithms require $O(m + n\log n)$ rounds and $\max(Δ, \log n) \log n$ bits. Our results significantly improve <span class="search-hit mathjax">memory</span> efficiency and time, achieving nearly linear-time leader election and MST. <span class="search-hit mathjax">Agents</span> are assumed to know $λ$, the maximum identifier, bounded by a polynomial in $n$.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2506.19365v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2506.19365v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

    <p class="is-size-7"><span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Submitted</span> 24 June, 2025; 
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">originally announced</span> June 2025.
      
    </p>
    

    

    
